window.docContents[228].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___18-Inequalities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___18-Inequalities--___4___18-Inequalities":{"contents":"The first four displays are real-variable inequalities, with every endpoint and\nstrictness condition retained.  Displays 4.18.5–4.18.10 are complex estimates;\nthe formal statements use the complex norm and make the real coordinates or\nsmall-disc hypothesis explicit.  The normalized sine in 4.18.3 is totalized at\nzero by its continuous value 1.\n\n\\frac{2x}{\\pi}\\le\\sin x\\le x,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$Jordan's inequality sandwiches the real sine between its chord lower bound and\nthe tangent-line upper bound.  Both endpoints are included.\n\nx\\le\\tan x,\\qquad 0\\le x<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$The tangent lower bound is an equality at x=0 and is strict for\n0<x<π/2; the pole π/2 is excluded from the domain rather than treated as\nan endpoint value.\n\n\\cos x\\le\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\le1,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le\\pi.$$At x=0, the quotient is understood by continuity as 1; the named sinc\ndefinition makes that endpoint convention explicit.  On the rest of the closed\ninterval the displayed quotient is ordinary division.\n\n\\pi<\\frac{\\sin(\\pi x)}{x(1-x)}\\le4,\\qquad0<x<1.$$The lower constant is strict throughout the open interval.  The upper\nequality is attained at the interior point x=1/2, while the lower bound has\nendpoint limit π; the formal statement preserves the strict/lax pair exactly.\n\n|\\sinh y|\\le|\\sin z|\\le\\cosh y,\\qquad z=x+iy.$$For complex z written with real coordinates x and y, the imaginary\ncoordinate controls the lower bound and the real hyperbolic cosine controls the\nupper bound.\n\n|\\sinh y|\\le|\\cos z|\\le\\cosh y,\\qquad z=x+iy.$$The corresponding cosine estimate has the same coordinate-dependent lower and\nupper bounds.\n\n|\\csc z|\\le\\operatorname{csch}|y|,\\qquad z=x+iy.$$The cosecant estimate is used away from the real axis, where the right side is\nfinite.  The local csch definition is the reciprocal of real hyperbolic sine;\nthe explicit y ≠ 0 hypothesis records this domain.\n\n|\\cos z|\\le\\cosh|z|.$$The global complex cosine norm is bounded by the real hyperbolic cosine of the\ncomplex modulus.\n\n|\\sin z|\\le\\sinh|z|.$$Likewise, the complex sine norm is bounded by the real hyperbolic sine of the\nmodulus.\n\n|\\cos z|<2,\\qquad |\\sin z|\\le\\frac65|z|,\\qquad |z|<1.$$Inside the unit disk the cosine has the strict bound 2, while sine has the\nexplicit displayed linear constant 6/5.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.18 Inequalities","header":"§4.18 Inequalities","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___18-Inequalities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___18-Inequalities--___4___18-Inequalities"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--Reflection-formulas--4___37___10-___--arcsinh_LPAR_-z_RPAR____-arcsinh-z-___":{"contents":"Source: DLMF 4.37.10\n\\mathrm{arcsinh}\\left(-z\\right)=-\\mathrm{arcsinh}z.$$\n### 4.37.11 — arccosh(-z)=±πi+arccosh z, Im z≷0.\n\nSource: DLMF 4.37.11\n\\mathrm{arccosh}\\left(-z\\right)=\\pm\\pi\\mathrm{i}+\\mathrm{arccosh}z,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z\\gtrless0.$$\n### 4.37.12 — arctanh(-z)=-arctanh z, z≠±1.\n\nSource: DLMF 4.37.12\n\\mathrm{arctanh}\\left(-z\\right)=-\\mathrm{arctanh}z,\\qquad z\\ne\\pm1.$$\n### 4.37.13 — arccsch(-z)=-arccsch z.\n\nSource: DLMF 4.37.13\n\\mathrm{arccsch}\\left(-z\\right)=-\\mathrm{arccsch}z.$$\n### 4.37.14 — arcsech(-z)=∓πi+arcsech z, Im z≷0.\n\nSource: DLMF 4.37.14\n\\mathrm{arcsech}\\left(-z\\right)=\\mp\\pi\\mathrm{i}+\\mathrm{arcsech}z,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z\\gtrless0.$$\n### 4.37.15 — arccoth(-z)=-arccoth z, z≠±1.\n\nSource: DLMF 4.37.15\n\\mathrm{arccoth}\\left(-z\\right)=-\\mathrm{arccoth}z,\\qquad z\\ne\\pm1.$$\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.37 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions\u0009§4.37 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions\u0009Reflection formulas","header":"4.37.10 —  arcsinh(-z)=-arcsinh z .","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--___4___37-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions--Reflection-formulas--4___37___10-___--arcsinh_LPAR_-z_RPAR____-arcsinh-z-___"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___13-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___13-Integrals--___5___13_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Barnes___-Beta-Integral":{"contents":"\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\n\\Gamma(a+it)\\Gamma(b+it)\\Gamma(c-it)\\Gamma(d-it)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n=\\frac{\\Gamma(a+c)\\Gamma(a+d)\\Gamma(b+c)\\Gamma(b+d)}\n{\\Gamma(a+b+c+d)},\\qquad\\Re a,\\Re b,\\Re c,\\Re d>0.All four parameters may be complex. BarnesBetaDomain carries the source's four\npositive real-part conditions. Its named normalized integral, convergence declaration,\nand value identity are separate objects.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.13 Integrals","header":"§5.13(i) Barnes' Beta Integral","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___13-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___13-Integrals--___5___13_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Barnes___-Beta-Integral"}});